capabilities of the new campaign should provide greater efficiency. Without this

243
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:30,920
media volume, we simply won't achieve the necessary market penetration to hit the 10%

244
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:31,920
sales target.

245
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:35,580
Let's look at the Creative Costs line item.

246
00:17:35,580 --> 00:17:40,080
This is also up significantly, close to 18%.

247
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:45,050
Is there an opportunity to streamline the production process here?

248
00:17:45,050 --> 00:17:50,450
We are using a new agency for the relaunch assets, which accounts for the higher

249
00:17:50,450 --> 00:17:51,930
initial rate.

250
00:17:51,930 --> 00:17:57,850
However, I do believe there's some flexibility in this area.

251
00:17:57,850 --> 00:18:00,830
Flexibility is exactly what I'm looking for, Maya.

252
00:18:00,830 --> 00:18:05,450
The finance team's objective is to maintain financial discipline.

253
00:18:05,450 --> 00:18:09,770
Are there specific areas within creative production where we could negotiate better

254
00:18:09,770 --> 00:18:14,800
terms or perhaps leverage existing internal assets?

255
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:16,520
That's a fair challenge, Ravi.

256
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:21,600
I've already flagged the agency contract terms for reevaluation.

257
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:28,680
We might be able to reduce the creative costs by 3% or 4% through stronger negotiation on

258
00:18:28,680 --> 00:18:34,020
fees and bundling the digital assets into one package.

259
00:18:34,020 --> 00:18:39,600
That 3-4% reduction would be helpful, but it still leaves a considerable gap against

260
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:42,060
our cost control mandate.

261
00:18:42,060 --> 00:18:47,610
We need to get closer to a figure that the company can sustainably support.

262
00:18:47,610 --> 00:18:51,090
Cutting the media spend is extremely difficult.

263
00:18:51,090 --> 00:18:57,290
If we reduce that allocation, we risk the entire campaign falling flat due to insufficient

264
00:18:57,290 --> 00:18:59,210
frequency and reach.

265
00:18:59,210 --> 00:19:05,250
That would make the whole investment unproductive.

266
00:19:05,250 --> 00:19:07,260
Good morning, Aisha.

267
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:11,380
Thanks for meeting with me today to discuss the financial risks we're facing with the

268
00:19:11,380 --> 00:19:16,500
expansion of global harvest exports into new international markets.

269
00:19:16,500 --> 00:19:20,100
I know you've had a chance to look at the preliminary reports.

270
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:21,780
Good morning, Samir.

271
00:19:21,780 --> 00:19:22,780
Absolutely.

272
00:19:22,780 --> 00:19:27,700
I've reviewed the financials and while the growth in sales is impressive, you're right.

273
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:30,380
The exposure has also increased.

274
00:19:30,380 --> 00:19:37,340
We need to get a firm grip on three key areas, currency risk, credit risk, and transit risk.

275
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:38,820
Exactly.

276
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:43,500
Let's start with the most immediate threat, currency fluctuation.

277
00:19:43,500 --> 00:19:49,100
Our contracts are primarily denominated in euros and yen, but our operating costs

278
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:52,460
here in Sri Lanka are in local currency.

279
00:19:52,460 --> 00:19:58,420
The recent weakening of the euro is already impacting our profit margins significantly.

280
00:19:58,420 --> 00:20:01,720
That's a classic translation risk for an export business.

281
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:07,020
The drop in the euro's value means less local currency revenue for the same foreign currency

282
00:20:07,020 --> 00:20:08,100
price.

283
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:12,660
We need to move away from simply reacting to the market and establish a formal hedging

284
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:13,660
strategy.

285
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:15,460
I agree completely.

286
00:20:15,460 --> 00:20:18,620
I've been exploring a few hedging instruments.

287
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:23,700
What is your professional recommendation for stabilizing our cash flows over the next six

288
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:24,700
months?

289
00:20:24,700 --> 00:20:29,900
Given the nature of our sales cycle and the relatively predictable revenue stream, forward

290
00:20:29,900 --> 00:20:33,940
contracts would be the most straightforward and effective tool.

291
00:20:33,940 --> 00:20:40,020
They allow us to lock in an exchange rate today for a future Euro Yen transaction, essentially

292
00:20:40,020 --> 00:20:42,700
removing the exchange rate uncertainty.

293
00:20:42,700 --> 00:20:44,780
That sounds like a solid plan.

294
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:51,380
I propose we engage with our bank to secure six-month forward contracts to cover approximately

295
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:56,060
75% of our forecasted euro and yen receivables.

296
00:20:56,060 --> 00:21:01,820
This provides stability while leaving a small portion open for potential favorable movements.

297
00:21:01,820 --> 00:21:05,220
That is a well-judged mitigation level, Samir.

298
00:21:05,220 --> 00:21:09,420
Now, let's pivot to the second major issue, credit risk.

299
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:13,820
The sales team has secured those two significant new customers overseas, but their credit

300
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:17,090
Histories are limited.

301
00:21:17,090 --> 00:21:20,730
The accounts receivable team flag the same concern.

302
00:21:20,730 --> 00:21:22,890
The potential revenue is huge,

303
00:21:22,890 --> 00:21:26,490
but the danger of delayed payments or worse default

304
00:21:26,490 --> 00:21:28,410
is a genuine worry.

305
00:21:28,410 --> 00:21:30,370
We can't afford a major write-off

306
00:21:30,370 --> 00:21:32,890
so early into this expansion phase.

307
00:21:32,890 --> 00:21:36,690
My immediate recommendation is a two-pronged approach.

308
00:21:36,690 --> 00:21:39,370
Firstly, we need to run a more rigorous,

309
00:21:39,370 --> 00:21:41,570
in-depth credit scoring assessment

310
00:21:41,570 --> 00:21:43,810
through an international credit agency

311
00:21:43,810 --> 00:21:46,090
going beyond the preliminary checks.

312
00:21:46,090 --> 00:21:48,250
Secondly, we must secure our position

313
00:21:48,250 --> 00:21:50,290
with trade credit insurance.

314
00:21:50,290 --> 00:21:52,610
Trade credit insurance.

315
00:21:52,610 --> 00:21:55,770
Can you elaborate on how that would work in this scenario?

316
00:21:55,770 --> 00:21:56,730
Certainly.

317
00:21:56,730 --> 00:21:59,770
It protects us from both protracted default,

318
00:21:59,770 --> 00:22:01,930
where the buyer takes too long to pay,

319
00:22:01,930 --> 00:22:05,170
and insolvency, where the buyer goes bankrupt.

320
00:22:05,170 --> 00:22:06,730
If a covered loss occurs,

321
00:22:06,730 --> 00:22:09,690
the insurer compensates us for a significant percentage

322
00:22:09,690 --> 00:22:11,450
of the unpaid invoice.

323
00:22:11,450 --> 00:22:13,450
It essentially transfers the credit risk

324
00:22:13,450 --> 00:22:15,130
off our balance sheet.

325
00:22:15,130 --> 00:22:19,850
That level of security is necessary for such high-value new contracts.

326
00:22:19,850 --> 00:22:23,990
It's a cost, but it's an investment in risk transference.

327
00:22:23,990 --> 00:22:26,910
Beyond insurance, what about payment terms?

328
00:22:26,910 --> 00:22:28,350
We must tighten them.

329
00:22:28,350 --> 00:22:32,590
For new clients with no established payment track record, we should insist on either

330
00:22:32,590 --> 00:22:37,820
a partial advance payment, say 20% upon contract signing, or sign...

331
00:22:37,820 --> 00:22:39,540
Good morning, Aisha.

332
00:22:39,540 --> 00:22:44,460
I'm glad we could schedule this follow-up after reviewing the risk assessment matrix

333
00:22:44,460 --> 00:22:46,900
we discussed last week.

334
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:48,540
Good morning, Samir.

335
00:22:48,540 --> 00:22:56,220
The data confirms our initial concerns, particularly regarding the volatility in the currency markets

336
00:22:56,220 --> 00:23:01,220
and the high credit risk associated with the new overseas accounts.

337
00:23:01,220 --> 00:23:04,980
Let's focus on the currency exposure first.

338
00:23:04,980 --> 00:23:11,880
As you know, the fluctuation in the euro and yen against the local currency is eroding our

339
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:14,060
profitability.

340
00:23:14,060 --> 00:23:18,900
Even small shifts are having a significant impact on our earnings.

341
00:23:18,900 --> 00:23:20,060
Correct.

342
00:23:20,060 --> 00:23:27,180
The immediate priority is locking in a predictable exchange rate for our future cash flows.

343
00:23:27,180 --> 00:23:31,580
Have you contacted the bank regarding the forward contracts we proposed?

344
00:23:31,580 --> 00:23:33,260
Yes, I have.

345
00:23:33,260 --> 00:23:40,100
The bank confirmed they can structure a six-month forward agreement covering up to 80% of our

346
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:43,520
known foreign currency receivables.

347
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:47,140
My initial proposal was 75%.

348
00:23:47,140 --> 00:23:51,260
Do you think 80% is an appropriate hedge ratio?

349
00:23:51,260 --> 00:23:57,900
Given the current market instability, increasing the coverage to 80% seems prudent.

350
00:23:57,900 --> 00:24:03,900
It provides maximum certainty for our budgeting process without completely eliminating the

351
00:24:03,900 --> 00:24:07,980
chance to benefit from an unexpected favorable movement.

352
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:11,990
I'll execute those contracts immediately then.

353
00:24:11,990 --> 00:24:14,890
That addresses the translation risk.

354
00:24:14,890 --> 00:24:20,170
Next, the credit risk raised by the two new large customers.

355
00:24:20,170 --> 00:24:25,390
The promise of high revenue is offset by their limited payment history.

356
00:24:25,390 --> 00:24:27,170
This is critical.

357
00:24:27,170 --> 00:24:33,770
The accounts receivable team has completed the detailed credit scoring assessment as requested.

358
00:24:33,770 --> 00:24:41,750
Both buyers fall into a medium high risk bracket due to short operational history and high leverage.

359
00:24:41,750 --> 00:24:44,210
That confirms our fears.

360
00:24:44,210 --> 00:24:51,440
We absolutely need to implement risk mitigation strategies before confirming those contracts.

361
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:55,800
Did you prepare a draft proposal for trade credit insurance?

362
00:24:55,800 --> 00:25:01,800
Yes, I've sourced quotes for a policy that would cover both the new clients.

363
00:25:01,800 --> 00:25:08,400
The premium is justifiable as it secures us against both protracted non-payment and outright

364
00:25:08,400 --> 00:25:09,400
insolvency.

365
00:25:09,400 --> 00:25:13,520
It's an essential risk transference mechanism.

366
00:25:13,520 --> 00:25:14,880
I agree.

367
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:19,960
It protects our balance sheet from a catastrophic client default.

368
00:25:19,960 --> 00:25:25,360
Beyond the insurance, we also discussed implementing stricter payment terms.

369
00:25:25,360 --> 00:25:30,800
How should we communicate this to the sales team without jeopardizing the deals?

370
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:36,720
We need to frame it as standard risk governance for high-value initial orders.

371
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:42,600
We should insist on a minimum 20% advance payment upon order confirmation.

372
00:25:42,600 --> 00:25:44,640
Additionally, the remaining balance...

373
00:25:44,640 --> 00:25:45,640
Good morning.

374
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:50,280
I wanted to discuss the capital budgeting proposal for Project NOVA.

375
00:25:50,280 --> 00:25:55,910
We need to finalize our financial strategy before presenting to the board next week.

376
00:25:55,910 --> 00:25:56,910
Good morning.

377
00:25:56,910 --> 00:25:57,910
Absolutely.

378
00:25:57,910 --> 00:26:02,790
I've compiled the initial cash flow projections and the sensitivity analysis based on the

379
00:26:02,790 --> 00:26:04,870
