730
00:50:39,230 --> 00:50:44,990
This translates directly into a higher cost overruns and schedule delays, which could

731
00:50:44,990 --> 00:50:48,570
completely erode that NPV advantage.

732
00:50:48,570 --> 00:50:51,950
I acknowledge the higher operational risk, Arjun.

733
00:50:51,950 --> 00:50:57,430
We're entering a new product space, but I believe focusing too heavily on short-term

734
00:50:57,430 --> 00:51:02,790
financial safety with Project Beta would be a strategic mistake.

735
00:51:02,790 --> 00:51:05,310
Why do you view it as a mistake?

736
00:51:05,310 --> 00:51:11,670
Project Beta offers a guaranteed positive steady cash flow and a quick launch utilizing

737
00:51:11,670 --> 00:51:13,710
existing resources.

738
00:51:13,710 --> 00:51:17,840
It's the definition of a safe, profitable bet.

739
00:51:17,840 --> 00:51:23,880
Because the tech industry is pivoting hard toward automated cloud-based solutions.

740
00:51:23,880 --> 00:51:29,360
Project Alpha isn't just a product, it's a strategic positioning move.

741
00:51:29,360 --> 00:51:35,040
Being an early mover in that market segment, small retail, gives Novatech a huge competitive

742
00:51:35,040 --> 00:51:38,000
advantage for long-term growth.

743
00:51:38,000 --> 00:51:42,600
Competitive advantage at a higher cost of capital and higher risk.

744
00:51:42,600 --> 00:51:48,400
My forecast shows the payback period for alpha is almost twice as long as betas.

745
00:51:48,400 --> 00:51:53,550
That ties up capital for a longer duration, limiting our flexibility.

746
00:51:53,550 --> 00:51:56,510
But consider the market expansion.

747
00:51:56,510 --> 00:52:00,870
Project beta simply upgrades our current customer base.

748
00:52:00,870 --> 00:52:04,310
Alpha opens up an entirely new market segment.

749
00:52:04,310 --> 00:52:09,590
If we miss this window, a competitor will surely step in and our future competitiveness

750
00:52:09,590 --> 00:52:11,110
will suffer.

751
00:52:11,110 --> 00:52:13,790
I agree that the market is shifting.

752
00:52:13,790 --> 00:52:19,590
That's why I ran a deeper analysis on the expected cash flows under a scenario of development

753
00:52:19,590 --> 00:52:20,790
delay.

754
00:52:20,790 --> 00:52:27,230
If the alpha launch is delayed by six months, the NPV drops significantly, though it remains

755
00:52:27,230 --> 00:52:28,350
positive.

756
00:52:28,350 --> 00:52:33,320
It just validates that we have zero margin for error on execution.

757
00:52:33,320 --> 00:52:36,000
That brings us back to the risk mitigation plan.

758
00:52:36,000 --> 00:52:39,000
We can address that execution risk directly.

759
00:52:39,000 --> 00:52:42,240
What if we structure the investment into phased tranches?

760
00:52:42,240 --> 00:52:48,680
We release capital for development only upon hitting specific, rigorous performance checkpoints.

761
00:52:48,680 --> 00:52:52,400
That's a sensible approach to managing the implementation risk.

762
00:52:52,400 --> 00:52:57,800
We can use the initial phase to onboard the core engineering team and validate the

763
00:52:57,800 --> 00:53:01,400
minimum viable product, MVP design.

764
00:53:01,400 --> 00:53:05,520
But what about the raw material volatility, the human capital?

765
00:53:05,520 --> 00:53:11,280
Alpha requires us to quickly hire and integrate specialized cloud engineers.

766
00:53:11,280 --> 00:53:14,610
Our human resources strategy.

767
00:53:14,610 --> 00:53:16,590
Good morning, Maya.

768
00:53:16,590 --> 00:53:20,660
Thanks for coming in to discuss the Q1 budget forecast.

769
00:53:20,660 --> 00:53:24,170
I appreciate you walking me through the numbers.

770
00:53:24,170 --> 00:53:26,100
Good morning, Ravi.

771
00:53:26,100 --> 00:53:32,030
I'm happy to go over the line item increases for the next fiscal year.

772
00:53:32,030 --> 00:53:36,310
I know the overall figure is noticeably fire.

773
00:53:36,310 --> 00:53:37,940
It is.

774
00:53:37,940 --> 00:53:44,180
The draft forecast shows a 15% increase in the total marketing spend.

775
00:53:44,180 --> 00:53:49,380
Given the company's current focus on strict cost control targets and rising production

776
00:53:49,380 --> 00:53:53,900
costs, I need a deeper understanding of this jump.

777
00:53:53,900 --> 00:53:57,180
I fully understand the pressure on costs.

778
00:53:57,180 --> 00:54:03,940
However, this increase is directly linked to the company's 10% revenue growth target.

779
00:54:03,940 --> 00:54:05,860
This isn't maintenance spending.

780
00:54:05,860 --> 00:54:10,160
It's an investment into major Q2 initiatives.

781
00:54:10,160 --> 00:54:12,880
Can you elaborate on those initiatives?

782
00:54:12,880 --> 00:54:18,880
Specifically, how do they justify an increase that is disproportionate to the anticipated

783
00:54:18,880 --> 00:54:21,550
10% revenue growth?

784
00:54:21,550 --> 00:54:22,950
Certainly.

785
00:54:22,950 --> 00:54:28,950
The biggest allocation is for the product relaunch and the major digital campaign planned

786
00:54:28,950 --> 00:54:31,060
for Q2.

787
00:54:31,060 --> 00:54:36,700
These require significant funds for media placements, high-quality creative production,

788
00:54:36,700 --> 00:54:38,860
and platform advertising.

789
00:54:38,860 --> 00:54:42,940
I see the planned increase in digital media spend here.

790
00:54:42,940 --> 00:54:46,380
It's up 22% year-over-year.

791
00:54:46,380 --> 00:54:52,500
While I recognize the need to drive awareness, we must ensure that every dollar is optimized

792
00:54:52,500 --> 00:54:55,460
for return on investment.

793
00:54:55,460 --> 00:55:01,140
We've modeled the ROI based on last year's conversion rates, and the enhanced targeting

794
00:55:01,140 --> 00:55:06,460
capabilities of the new campaign should provide greater efficiency.

795
00:55:06,460 --> 00:55:11,740
Without this media volume, we simply won't achieve the necessary market penetration to

796
00:55:11,740 --> 00:55:14,580
hit the 10% sales target.

797
00:55:14,580 --> 00:55:17,580
Let's look at the creative costs line item.

798
00:55:17,580 --> 00:55:22,060
This is also up significantly, close to 18%.

799
00:55:22,060 --> 00:55:27,020
Is there an opportunity to streamline the production process here?

800
00:55:27,020 --> 00:55:32,980
We are using a new agency for the relaunch assets, which accounts for the higher initial

801
00:55:32,980 --> 00:55:33,980
rate.

802
00:55:33,980 --> 00:55:39,820
However, I do believe there's some flexibility in this area.

803
00:55:39,820 --> 00:55:42,820
Flexibility is exactly what I'm looking for, Maya.

804
00:55:42,820 --> 00:55:47,420
The finance team's objective is to maintain financial discipline.

805
00:55:47,420 --> 00:55:51,740
Are there specific areas within creative production where we could negotiate better

806
00:55:51,740 --> 00:55:56,780
terms or perhaps leverage existing internal assets?

807
00:55:56,780 --> 00:55:58,580
That's a fair challenge, Ravi.

808
00:55:58,580 --> 00:56:03,580
I've already flagged the agency contract terms for re-evaluation.

809
00:56:03,580 --> 00:56:10,660
We might be able to reduce the creative costs by 3% or 4% through stronger negotiation on

810
00:56:10,660 --> 00:56:16,020
fees and bundling the digital assets into one package.

811
00:56:16,020 --> 00:56:21,580
That 3-4% reduction would be helpful, but it still leaves a considerable gap against

812
00:56:21,580 --> 00:56:24,060
our cost control mandate.

813
00:56:24,060 --> 00:56:29,600
We need to get closer to a figure that the company can sustainably support.

814
00:56:29,600 --> 00:56:33,080
Cutting the media spend is extremely difficult.

815
00:56:33,080 --> 00:56:39,300
If we reduce that allocation, we risk the entire campaign falling flat due to insufficient

816
00:56:39,300 --> 00:56:41,200
frequency and reach.

817
00:56:41,200 --> 00:56:43,480
That would make the whole investment unproductive.
